course: dictionary
lesson: 7831
title: Quote and quotation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To quote means to repeat the exact words someone else said or wrote. The words you repeat are the quote. «"It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel» gives the real words of a real person, and that makes a news report feel true.
explain: In English lessons you quote from a text to support your answer. Copy the exact words, put them in quotation marks, and say where they come from. Your quote proves your point, so choose it carefully.
explain: Quote is a word with more than one meaning. A builder who gives you a quote is telling you how much a job will cost. In maths, a quote is a price you can compare with another price.
explain: Be careful online. «When a bot pretends to be a pirate or a footballer, it is still pretending; quotes it invents were never really said.» Always find the real source before you trust a quote.
fact: To quote is to repeat the exact words someone else said or wrote.
fact: A quote is the exact words that are repeated.
fact: Quotation marks, written as " ", show where the quoted words start and stop.
fact: To quote a price means to say how much something will cost; a quote is that price.
fact: «Quote, unquote» is said to show that you are repeating someone's words.
exercise: In English lessons, pupils quote from a text to support their answers. What does quote mean here? | Draw a picture of the text | *Repeat the exact words from the text | Change the words into your own words | Read it out in a whisper
exercise: A builder says, «Here is my quote for the new garden wall.» What is a quote in this sentence? | A funny joke | A type of brick | *A price for the job | A set of quotation marks
exercise: In «"It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel», which words are the quote? | the whole first paragraph | Mrs Patel | *It was terrifying | said Mrs Patel
test: What does the phrase «quote, unquote» show? | That you are giving a price | *That you are repeating someone's words | That you are very angry | That you are whispering
test: Why should you check a quote from a bot? | Because bots never use quotation marks | Because bots only write in italics | Because bots always tell the truth | *Because a bot may invent quotes that were never really said
exam: Why is a real quote useful in a news report? | It hides who wrote the report | It makes the report longer for no reason | It shows the price of a job | *It gives the real words of a real person and makes the report feel true
